        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Uncommon in both dogs and cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "No breed or sex predilection.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Age range at time of diagnosis, 1–13 years.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acidemia may cause lethargy, muscle weakness (may be due to hypokalemia), inappetence, nausea, weight loss, stunted growth, and neurologic signs.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Other signs depend on the associated diseases (e.g., pyelonephritis).",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Panting.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Polyuria and polydipsia usually associated with hypokalemia or calciuresis.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vomiting.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hematuria and dysuria secondary to urolithiasis.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Osteomalacia associated with chronic metabolic acidosis.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May be primary (i.e., inherited), or secondary to hypercalciuria, toxins, drugs (e.g., amphotericin B), altered calcium metabolism causing nephrocalcinosis (e.g., hypervitaminosis D, primary hyperparathyroidism), autoimmune (e.g., immunemediated hemolytic anemia, systemic lupus erythematosus [SLE], renal transplant rejection), hypergammaglobulinemic disorders (e.g., multiple myeloma, SLE), and tubulointerstitial nephropathies.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In cats, distal RTA has been associated with pyelonephritis, hepatic lipidosis, idiopathic with secondary hyperaldosteronism, and topical ophthalmic carbonic anhydrase inhibitor.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In dogs, clinical cases were idiopathic or associated with immune-mediated hemolytic anemia, leptospirosis, gastric-dilationvolvulus, zonisamide therapy, or experimentally induced renal ischemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Consider other causes of hyperchloremic normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (e.g., diarrhea, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, ammonium chloride, cationic amino acids, post-hypocapnic metabolic acidosis, dilutional acidosis, hypoadrenocorticism). Small bowel diarrhea is the most important differential.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Results vary depending on associated diseases.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypokalemia due to increased renal excretion.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Alkaluria (pH >6.0); rule out ureasepositive urinary tract infection.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Venous blood gas and serum electrolyte analysis indicates hyperchloremic normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. Urine pH is >6.0 in distal RTA vs. <5.5 in proximal RTA.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "IMAGING",
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Radiography—may detect uroliths or osteomalacia.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The key diagnostic feature is normal anion gap metabolic acidosis accompanied by an inappropriately alkaline urine pH (>6.0).",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ammonium chloride tolerance test— administer 200 mg/kg PO; measure urine pH before and hourly for 5 hours; empty the bladder hourly. Urine pH in normal dogs decreases to <5.5 within 4 hours. Avoid this test in severe acidemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Type 1 and 2 RTA can be differentiated based on response to sodium bicarbonate infusion (0.5–1.0 mEq/kg/h). Fractional excretion of bicarbonate will increase markedly in type 2 RTA.",
              "pdf_page": 1269,
              "printed_page": 1187,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": []
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Individualize depending on the nature and severity of associated conditions.",
          "pdf_page": 1269,
          "printed_page": 1187,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Less bicarbonate is needed to resolve metabolic acidosis caused by distal RTA than is needed with proximal RTA.",
          "pdf_page": 1269,
          "printed_page": 1187,
          "column": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Hypokalemia may resolve with bicarbonate or citrate administration, but additional potassium supplementation may be required.",
          "pdf_page": 1269,
          "printed_page": 1187,
          "column": 2
        }
      ],
